# =============================================================================
# example.com-trademark — canonical environment contract (.env.example)
# =============================================================================
# The prelim-search product reads all of its configuration + credentials from
# the environment. In production these live in the systemd EnvironmentFile
# (`~/.env`, loaded as whichever service account runs the engine); in dev they are
# exported before the run. This file is the DOCUMENTED CONTRACT — copy it to
# `.env`, fill in the real secrets, and leave the non-secret defaults as shown.
#
# GOVERNANCE: the complete cross-process inventory (the MCP faces and admin services included), the
# ownership tiers, and the drift/rotation map live in docs/architecture/05-config-governance.md.
# `node scripts/env-audit.mjs` prints the live counts; this file no longer carries a figure of its own,
# because the one it did carry ("~186") was written once and never moved again.
#
# Removed as dead 2026-07-19: CLEAROTRON_PRICING_JSON,
# CLEAROTRON_NO_WRITE_DISCIPLINE, CLEAROTRON_FRAME_REOPEN_FRESH (zero code reads). Removed 2026-08-03:
# CLEAROTRON_AGENT (zero code reads — the run agent comes from CLEAROTRON_DEFAULT_AGENT). RETIRED 2026-07-27
# and never listed here: CLEAROTRON_KNOCKOUT_MODE, CLEAROTRON_JX_LANES, CLEAROTRON_RECIPES_MODE — if any of the
# three is still in your .env, delete the line; the pipeline CLI warns on stderr when it sees one.
#
# WHERE THE REST WENT (#1838). This file used to carry 237 rows and a new reader met 230 variables to
# reach the fourteen they need. The deployment and tuning names — identity, ports, tenancy, timeouts,
# batch sizes — now live in `.env.deployment.example`. Nothing was dropped: `node scripts/env-audit.mjs`
# reads BOTH files, and the ratchet that refuses a product variable with no row anywhere reads both too.
#
# CONVENTIONS
#   • Secrets show a SAFE placeholder ("changeme", "sk-...", "your-...-key").
#     NEVER commit a real secret here.
#   • Non-secrets (URLs, ports, hosts, model ids, environment flags) show their
#     REAL in-code default as the value, so this doubles as a defaults reference.
#   • Every var is overridable; unset ⇒ the in-code fallback shown in the comment.
#   • ON/OFF IDIOM: a behavioural gate is DEFAULT ON and the literal `0` disables it — that holds for
#     EVERY gate below, and it is the spelling to use. An opt-in is DEFAULT OFF and `1` arms it.
#     Empty always means "not set", never "on".
#   • `off`/`false`/`no` are additionally accepted by exactly FIVE vars — CLEAROTRON_PLAN_DISPATCH,
#     CLEAROTRON_DUMP_JSON, CLEAROTRON_FORM_REPAIR, which read through
#     envGateOn/envOn in driver/driver.config.mjs, plus the
#     same predicate in the shared identifier scanner (shared/ must not import driver/). Every
#     other gate is an inline `!== "0"` and treats
#     `off` as a non-empty string, i.e. as ON. Write `0`, not `off`, unless the var's own comment says
#     otherwise.
#   • Where a var takes a NAMED MODE, the comment says so and no other value does anything at all
#     (CLEAROTRON_SCREEN_GATE_UNNAMED = "enforce"; CLEAROTRON_UNREACHABLE_SENIOR = open-item|clamp).
#   • `# effect: <class>` above a row DECLARES WHAT CHANGES when that name is changed (#1149-12,
#     ADR-0002). The vocabulary is closed — silent-output-change | disclosed-gate | credential |
#     deployment | tuning | harness — and EFFECT_CLASSES in scripts/env-audit.mjs says what each one
#     claims. Like `# external:`, the marker must be the LAST comment above its rows and reaches the
#     contiguous run below it. The environment-governance guard reds on a NEW `CLEAROTRON_*` read by
#     product code that declares nothing; 96 existing names are a frozen backlog, not a pass. The
#     declaration records what a class IS, and is not a claim that it is harmless — the guard checks
#     the string, a reader checks the truth.
#   • "Reconstructed" / "verify at deploy" notes flag values that could not be
#     confirmed inside this repo (see AZURE_OPENAI_* below).
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. ENGINE / RUNTIME  (the deterministic driver — driver/driver.config.mjs)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ── Whether the copy of this file is read AT ALL (shared/env-local.mjs) ───────────────────────────
# Set it to 1 and the eight CLI entry points do NOT read `<repo>/.env`; every value must then arrive
# named, from the unit file or the supervisor. That is what a service box wants: a checkout's `.env`
# reaching a running service is a value nobody deployed.
#
# You mostly do not have to set it. systemd sets INVOCATION_ID on anything it starts, and the same
# module reads that to reach the same conclusion without configuration. Set this explicitly for a
# deployment systemd does not supervise — a container, another supervisor — and for a box where you
# want the statement to be readable rather than inferred.
#
# It is one of the few gates read BEFORE the alias layer runs, so both spellings work here; write the
# current one. Off-words (`0`/`off`/`false`/`no`) disable it; empty means not set.
#
# Compute engine = which PROVIDER ADAPTER runs the reasoning stages. Adapters implement one runTurn() contract
# (engine/CONTRACT.md) and are held in a registry; the driver is provider-agnostic, so provider choice is a
# registered adapter selected here — NOT a model-name swap. An unknown value fails loud. (The legacy
# gateway-bin adapter was a runtime, not a provider choice; removed in the extraction.)
#
# TWO adapters are implemented — engine/CONTRACT.md:3 is the accurate statement, and this file said
# otherwise until #547:
#   anthropic-agent  `claude -p`, off-gateway. The default.
#   openai-agent     `codex exec`. Built and $0-tested; live verification still pending.
# Either way the reasoning needs a subscription or an API key of its own — that is separate from every
# register credential below, and no register source removes it.
CLEAROTRON_AI=anthropic-agent
# Path/name of the engine binary — ONE PER ENGINE. Only the live engine's value is read, so a box that
# runs one engine sets one and a box that runs both sets both, to different paths. Give each an ABSOLUTE
# path if the binary is not on PATH: stage subprocesses run with cwd set to the run directory.
CLEAROTRON_CLAUDE_PATH=claude

# ── openai-agent (codex) knobs — ONLY read when CLEAROTRON_AI=openai-agent ────────────────────────
# The codex binary. Its OWN name now, so it can sit uncommented beside the claude row without either
# overwriting the other — these were one variable until that collapse met a box needing two different
# paths, and this file shipped two live rows for the single name until then (a last-wins assignment in
# the copied `.env`, not a choice).
CLEAROTRON_CODEX_PATH=codex
CLEAROTRON_WORK_DIR=
# Customer/project config store (loadProfiles). Unset ⇒ driver's built-in profiles dir.
CLEAROTRON_CUSTOMERS_DIR=
# Compute-skills tree the stage prompts resolve `skills/foo/SKILL.md` against. Default = <driverDir>/skills.
CLEAROTRON_INSTRUCTIONS_DIR=
# Delivery pool: report.html + audit.xlsx land here (served by Caddy behind Cloudflare Access).
# REQUIRED — #774 removed the code default. Unset, the engine refuses and names this variable instead
# of falling back to /srv/trademark-archive, which on a deployed box is real client matter.
CLEAROTRON_REPORTS_DIR=/srv/trademark-archive
# Run-slot lock dir. Default = $CLEAROTRON_WORK_DIR/prelim-run-locks. Every run takes a slot here,
# whether the runner dispatched it or somebody launched it by hand, so this is what bounds concurrency
# across an install rather than within one process. The portal also reads it to find the worker
# heartbeat (#1721); two installs pointed at one lock dir would share a cap they do not expect to.
# effect: deployment
CLEAROTRON_RUN_LOCK_DIR=
# Delivery outbox dir (instant handoff-mode delivery wake). Default = $CLEAROTRON_WORK_DIR/prelim-outbox.
# effect: deployment
CLEAROTRON_OUTBOX_DIR=
# HEADLESS intake: one explicit queue dir (no agent workspaces needed) — the enqueue CLI + ops-MCP
# start_run write here and the runner drains it (additive to the workspace scan). See docs/INTAKE.md.
CLEAROTRON_QUEUE_DIR=
# REQUIRED, on every deployment including dev (#503). Which register vendor gets called and billed:
# corsearch | clarivate | signa | euipo | uspto-local | free-tier. There is NO default — unset, the driver refuses at run start rather
# than choosing for you.
#
# It used to default to corsearch, and the comment here called this an override for "dev/test parity"
# while claiming production flipped a committed literal. Both were wrong: production has always run on
# this variable, and the fallback named a vendor the deployment did not choose. The real value lived
# in a systemd drop-in that is in neither box's ~/.env and not in git, so a rebuilt unit or a fresh box
# silently resolved to that vendor — with no error, because a default is the one wrong value that never
# announces itself.
#
# Set it HERE, in the environment the deploy carries, not in a drop-in on one machine.
#
# SIX VALUES, in two TIERS. Precedence runs between the tiers, never within one:
#
#   PAID    corsearch | clarivate | signa   global sweep, one subscription each. If a paid vendor is
#                                           configured it IS the register, alone — the free sources are
#                                           not run beside it (corsearch and clarivate already
#                                           aggregate both free offices, so a second call buys nothing).
#
#   FREE    euipo         the EU register ALONE (#546)      — needs EUIPO_CLIENT_ID + _SECRET
#           uspto-local   the US register ALONE (#547)      — needs USPTO_LOCAL_DB (a built index)
#           free-tier     BOTH, as ONE register (#548)      — needs all three of the above
#
# Choosing a free value makes every territory outside its coverage a DISCLOSED DEFERRED coverage row.
# That is the honest trade of running without a subscription, not a defect to work around.
#
# `free-tier` requires BOTH members and refuses by name without them — a half-configured composite
# would defer every EU+US slice whole and lose the coverage it does have. To run one office
# deliberately, name that office.
CLEAROTRON_DATABASE=clarivate

# Runtime-injected — do NOT set these by hand. The driver writes them per-run into the gather
# MCP server env (engine/mcp/gather-config.mjs) for telemetry attribution:
#   CLEAROTRON_GATHER_SESSION_KEY, CLEAROTRON_GATHER_SESSION_ID, CLEAROTRON_GATHER_AGENT,
#   CLEAROTRON_REGISTER_CALL_LOG, CLEAROTRON_REGISTER_RECORD_LOG
#
# THERE IS NO RETENTION SETTING AND NO CLEANUP JOB (#743). CLEAROTRON_REGISTER_LEDGER_MAX_BYTES,
# CLEAROTRON_REGISTER_LEDGER_KEEP and `npm run prune:ledger` are gone with the rotation tool, because the
# thing they bounded no longer grows: RECORD BODIES ARE WRITTEN INTO THE RUN'S OWN DIRECTORY
# (<run>/_driver/register-record-bodies.jsonl), archived with the run and purged with it. Nobody should
# have to install a timer to run a clearance engine, and an install that skipped it used to end up with
# a half-gigabyte file — production reached 432 MB in 61 days.
#
# CLEAROTRON_REGISTER_RECORD_LOG is therefore RUNTIME-INJECTED PER RUN, pointing into that run's directory.
# Setting it by hand pins every run's record bodies to one file and re-creates the problem.
#
# CLEAROTRON_REGISTER_CALL_LOG is the one that stays box-global: it is one small row per provider call, read
# ACROSS runs by the provider-usage tally, and it is the independent witness that a run made record
# fetches at all (so a run whose bodies went missing reports as a failure and not as a clean zero).
# Leave it unset: the default is ~/trademark/telemetry/register-calls.jsonl (#774 — an existing ledger
# under the pre-#774 telemetry directory keeps being read where it sits), and an existing corsearch-named
# file from before #594 is still read where it sits — production is on that filename today, and reading
# it is not a fault. The vendor-named ENV VARS are gone (#605).
#
# #1269 — BEING BOX-GLOBAL, IT USED TO COLLECT THE TEST SUITE'S FIXTURE TRAFFIC. A full-suite run on a
# dev or test box appended mock provider calls to this same file, and nothing on the rows said so, so
# every usage pattern, provider comparison and cost read over it silently included them. `npm test`
# now exports CLEAROTRON_SUITE_TELEMETRY_DIR into its own temp root and the ledgers resolve under that
# instead. It is set by the test wrapper and is NOT an operator setting: setting it by hand sends a
# real run's billing rows somewhere the tally never reads.
#
# A box upgraded across #743 still holds its old global RECORD file. Nothing writes or reads it now; the
# driver says so once per process on stderr. Archive it with one `mv` when convenient.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. MODEL / API CREDS  (Anthropic subscription/API + the Azure catalog id)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anthropic API key. NOTE: the anthropic-agent engine STRIPS this from the `claude -p`
# subprocess by default so it bills the OAuth SUBSCRIPTION (cheaper), not the API. Keep it set
# for the gateway + as the api-key fallback (see CLEAROTRON_AI_BILLING).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# The headless subscription sign-in's token (tracker issue 2070). `claude setup-token` — run on any
# machine you can sign in on — prints it; setup captures it by paste. The stage subprocess inherits it
# untouched in both billing modes (spawnEnv strips only the API key), so this row is the whole
# mechanism by which a browserless server authenticates the subscription lane.
# external: read by the claude CLI itself, not by this product's code — inheritance is the contract
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
# Auth mode for the engine: "subscription" (default) or "api-key". It applies to WHICHEVER engine
# CLEAROTRON_AI names, and each engine reads a DIFFERENT key under "api-key" — this is the pairing to
# get right, because the wrong one is refused at the run door rather than at setup:
#
#   anthropic-agent  →  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY   (subscription strips it, so the `claude -p` OAuth bills)
#   openai-agent     →  CODEX_API_KEY       (NOT OPENAI_API_KEY — the adapter strips that one for a
#                                            clean subscription bill, so it cannot serve as the key)
#
# `npm run setup` asks this as its own question, adopts the key, and proves the pair with one cheap
# turn before writing anything (#1149 item 5) — so a hand-edit here is the second-best route.
CLEAROTRON_AI_BILLING=subscription
# The api-key credential for openai-agent. Only read when CLEAROTRON_AI=openai-agent and
# CLEAROTRON_AI_BILLING=api-key; under "subscription" that engine reads ~/.codex/auth.json instead and
# refuses before spending if it is absent, so subscription is not the precondition-free option there.
CODEX_API_KEY=
# THE PRODUCT DELETES THIS ONE. Under CLEAROTRON_AI_BILLING=subscription the openai-agent adapter
# strips OPENAI_API_KEY from the `codex exec` environment, so a clean subscription bill cannot be
# spoiled by a key that happens to be exported on the box. It is listed because a reader who has one
# set needs to know it is removed, not because setting it here does anything: it never serves as the
# api-key credential — CODEX_API_KEY does.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# Azure OpenAI provider creds. RECONSTRUCTED: only $AZURE_OPENAI_DEPLOYMENT is referenced in this
# repo (as the rendered catalog id); these follow the azure-openai provider convention of the agent
# platform that consumes them, NOT this repo directly. Verify the exact names against that platform's
# own configuration at deploy — that config belongs to it and is not in this tree under any name (#876).
# There was never a reader HERE to retire, which is what separates these rows from a stranded one — and
# the line below is what tells the catalogue guard so (#1426).
# external: the agent platform's azure-openai provider config (#876)
AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY=changeme
AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=https://your-resource.openai.azure.com
AZURE_OPENAI_DEPLOYMENT=gpt-5.4
AZURE_OPENAI_API_VERSION=2024-10-21


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. REGISTER PROVIDERS  (ONE active at a time)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only the ACTIVE provider's credential is required at run start (preflightCredentials fails
# closed without it). The active provider is whatever CLEAROTRON_DATABASE names, in section 1 of this
# file — an ENV VAR, not a committed literal, and there is no literal under any spelling to edit.
#
# THERE IS NO DEFAULT PROVIDER, and this block said there was until now. Unset, CLEAROTRON_DATABASE
# resolves to null and every use of it throws at the run door. #503 removed the old default for the
# reason worth knowing before you leave it unset: an unset variable took Corsearch in silence, and the
# credential guard could not catch it because both boxes carried CORSEARCH_SESSION_KEY.
#
# ADR-0001 recommends Signa, not Corsearch: one self-serve key, US + EU + WIPO and eight more offices.
#
# Corsearch.
CORSEARCH_SESSION_KEY=changeme
# Clarivate Compumark.
CLARIVATE_API_KEY=changeme
# Signa.
SIGNA_API_KEY=changeme
#
# USPTO, local (#547) — the free US source, and the only one here that is not a paid vendor. TWO
# variables, and they are needed at different moments, which is why neither substitutes for the other.
#
# USPTO_LOCAL_DB — where the register FILE lives. The provider searches this; at SEARCH time there is no
# API and no key. Absolute path.
#
# It is a REQUIRED credential in every sense that matters: preflightCredentials reads this name and
# refuses the run when it is unset. An unconfigured local register does not fail at a wire the way a
# missing API key does — it would search nothing and report a clean US register.
USPTO_LOCAL_DB=/var/lib/cordillera/uspto/us.db
#
# USPTO_API_KEY — read by `node bin/uspto-sync.mjs` to DOWNLOAD the bulk data that builds that file.
# Never read at search time. Free: a USPTO.gov account with ID.me identity verification, then the key is
# on the account's Manage API Key page. Nothing is billed — the account exists to rate-limit the
# endpoint. It rides the X-API-KEY header.
#
# You can skip it entirely. `uspto-sync --from-file <archive>` ingests a bulk file you obtained some
# other way and needs no account at all; the index it builds carries no source date, so the provider
# refuses to count rather than answering zero. That refusal is deliberate, not a fault.
USPTO_API_KEY=changeme


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. RESEARCH  (common-law / web sweep)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Perplexity API key (perplexity_research MCP tool; common-law + synthesis gather stages).
#
# NEEDED BY EVERY PRODUCT — not an optional enrichment, which is what this file and INSTALL.md both
# used to imply. There are four products and all four use it, though they behave differently without
# it and the difference is worth knowing before you diagnose one:
#
#   Knockout search           RUNS ANYWAY, and says what it skipped (#1223 acceptance 6). The screen
#                             carries the register count probe, so its register half is a whole answer
#                             without this key: it delivers counts, marks every name for manual
#                             verification, and states on the report that the open-web half did not
#                             run. The sweep is NOT ATTEMPTED — nothing is billed and nothing 429s.
#                             CLEAROTRON_KNOCKOUT_SWEEP_FIXTURES still gives a $0 dev run WITH a sweep.
#   The three clearances      REFUSE AT PREFLIGHT, before a stage has spent (#1149 item 6). All three
#   (global preliminary,      carry `commonLawGrid: true` and a saved search cannot switch it off
#    multi-country focus,     (search-policy.mjs), so the deterministic grid sweep always runs, and the
#    full country)            run door refuses by name — grep the tree for the exact string:
#                             `[preflight] missing PERPLEXITY_API_KEY for the common-law / marketplace
#                             sweep on product` (driver.config.mjs, preflightResearchCredential). The
#                             throw lands before buildRunContext, so there is no run directory left
#                             behind to clean up.
#                             THIS MOVED. It used to fail at the common-law stage, after every register
#                             stage had been paid for, with "PERPLEXITY_API_KEY not set — marketplace
#                             research unavailable" and a "grid ledger never saved (machine-receipts
#                             contract)" throw. Same refusal, no spend in front of it — so if you are
#                             reading those older strings in a log, that run predates 2026-08-20.
#
# The engine does not quietly deliver a clearance with the unregistered-use half missing, which is the
# behaviour the word "optional" would lead you to expect.
PERPLEXITY_API_KEY=pplx-...

# SerpAPI key — the CHINESE MARKETPLACE half of the sweep (Baidu across Taobao, Tmall, JD, 1688,
# Pinduoduo, Xiaohongshu). Code-side search calls, no model in the data path.
#
# WHERE YOU GET IT: an account at serpapi.com. It is metered by search count — when the plan is
# exhausted the engine surfaces `SerpAPI 429: Your account has run out of searches`, which is a
# top-up somebody can do in five minutes rather than a defect to diagnose.
#
# WITHOUT IT the lane does not silently shrink: every grid cell gaps and the run says so, naming the
# dominant cause (`SERPAPI_API_KEY absent from driver env`). There is no switch to arm first — #1149
# item 8 deleted it — so any run whose product carries the zh lane needs this key or discloses the gap.
SERPAPI_API_KEY=your-serpapi-key


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. REGISTER / EU  (EUIPO — OAuth2 client-credentials)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Needed ONLY when CLEAROTRON_DATABASE=euipo (section 4). Until #546, these were read by a side
# tool that attached to every register stage whether or not they were set — so an instance with no
# EUIPO key looked identical to one with a key. EUIPO is a register provider now: unset credentials
# make a run REFUSE at preflight, by name, before any model spend.
#
# EUIPO OAuth client id + secret (client-credentials grant; the core owns token cache + refresh).
EUIPO_CLIENT_ID=your-euipo-client-id
EUIPO_CLIENT_SECRET=changeme
# EUIPO target environment: "sandbox" | "production".
#
# NOT A PREFERENCE. These are SEPARATE DEPLOYMENTS holding DIFFERENT CORPORA — sandbox is not a thin
# copy of production, it is another register. Every response stamps which host answered so a reader
# cannot mistake one for the other, and an unrecognised value is refused rather than defaulted.
# Production is the only correct value for real client work — so it is what this file now shows, per
# the convention in the header (a non-secret carries its real deployed value, not a safe-looking one).
# `sandbox` sat here as the example and is the wrong default to copy: it is a different register, and a
# clearance run against it returns a confident answer about the wrong corpus.
EUIPO_ENVIRONMENT=production


PROFILE_REPO_ROOT=
SIGNA_FIXTURES_DIR=             # canned signa payloads — the $0 seam for that adapter

# ── Names the audit could not see until tracker issue 1460 ───────────────────────────────────────
# Each of these is read through a name held in a string constant — `const IDENT = "NAME"` in one file,
# `process.env[IDENT]` in another. Both halves are literals at the author's keyboard, so they were
# always governed names; the scanner simply could not reach them. They are catalogued here now.

# Path to the built similar-group database the JX subclass lane reads (providers/jx-subclass/
# load-public.mjs builds it from the committed public tables). Unset ⇒ the lane refuses by name rather
# than answering from nothing. It names where the table lives; what the table SAYS is the build's.
#
# IT LIVES HERE DESPITE BEING AN OPERATOR'S CONCERN, and the reason is the other file's promise: the
# deployment reference opens by saying every name in it has a working default and a laptop user never
# sets one. This name has no default — unset, the lane refuses. Moving it there would spend that
# header's credibility, which every other row in that file depends on, to buy a tidier taxonomy.
# `.env.example` promises "what you decide at install", and a must-set variable fits deciding-at-install
# better than it fits never-set-one. Owner ruling, 2026-08-30, on tracker issue 1838.
#
# GIVING THE LANE A DEFAULT WAS CONSIDERED AND REFUSED: refuse-by-name-when-unset is the honest design —
# the same family as the pool root, where an unset value THROWS rather than quietly resolving somewhere
# real — and changing it is a product decision about rating behaviour, not a config-surface tidy-up.
# effect: deployment
CLEAROTRON_JX_SUBCLASS_DB=

# Redirects the provider telemetry ledger into a suite run's own temp root, so a suite never writes the
# box's ledger and never inherits it. Sits BELOW an explicitly-named ledger file: a test that names its
# own path is being deliberate, and this exists for the runs that name nothing.
#
# CANNOT LEAVE THIS FILE WHILE PRODUCT CODE READS IT (tracker issue 1838). The catalogue ratchet
# refuses any product-read name with no row, and the backlog it would otherwise join turns ONE WAY.
# So the remedy is the knobs step, not the surface step: remove the READ, not the row. A fixture
# seam reached through the environment is that argument in its purest form.
# Read by providers/_shared/ledger-path.mjs.
# effect: harness
CLEAROTRON_SUITE_TELEMETRY_DIR=

# The DEMO posture. Ordering is REAL: the four products are listed and orderable, the form, the plan and
# the confirmation are the product's own, and the confirmation resolves to a finished report that already
# exists rather than dispatching — no engine turn, no register call, no queue entry, no run directory. A
# product the demo carries no finished report for refuses and names which one. It also re-aims two boot
# warnings written for an operator of a real deployment at the visitor who is not one.
# `clearotron demo` sets this on the two processes that read it — it is not an operator knob, and it is
# listed here because the catalogue ratchet is about what an operator can FIND, not what they should set.
# Replaces PORTAL_DEMO, which named only one of the processes that has to know.
# Read by driver/portal-service.mjs, driver/demo-posture.mjs and mcp-server/http-server.mjs.
# Refs tracker issue 2015, 2106.
# effect: deployment
CLEAROTRON_DEMO=

# How many client what-ifs the runner drains at once (default 1). A what-if re-runs ONE stage of a run
# that already exists, in a sandbox, at a client's request — the owner opened it to clients on
# 2026-08-27 and ruled SPEND controls out with it ("ignore the call spend"). This is not one.
#
# It bounds CONCURRENCY, which is a different question the ruling did not touch: an account may queue as
# many experiments as it likes, and with no bound at all a client experimenting could occupy the box
# while a lawyer's paid clearance waits. The default of 1 says a what-if is background work.
#
# IT IS NOT A RUN-SLOT, deliberately. A what-if that took a slot from CLEAROTRON_MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS could
# BLOCK an admitted paid run rather than merely share the machine with it — the same starvation one
# layer up. So the two pools are separate and only this one bounds experiments.
# Read by driver/whatif-worker.mjs. Refs tracker issue 1953.
# effect: tuning
CLEAROTRON_WHATIF_MAX_CONCURRENT=1
